\section{What each model makes of each term}{

\code{tidy()} of the result has one row for each term of the formula, and
fits nothing. Its \code{status} is what the model makes of the term:
\itemize{
\item \code{"fits"}: the model fits the term as it is written;
\item \code{"adapted"}: the term is rewritten as its equivalent in the model, in \code{as};
\item \code{"interpreted"}: the model has a term of the same name that it defines
differently, as \code{reason} says;
\item \code{"warns"}: the model fits the term, but with a warning, as \code{reason} says;
\item \code{"drops"}: the model cannot fit the term, so it is left out, and the rest
is fitted;
\item \code{"refuses"}: the model aborts, as \code{reason} says.
}

An MRQAP warns for a term that reads the outcome's own ties, such as
\code{recip}, and refuses \code{tie()}, \code{indeg()}, and \code{outdeg()} of the outcome
layer. It drops an offset, such as goldfish's \code{offset(recip, coef = 0)},
since it estimates every coefficient; ergm keeps an ergm one. For an MRQAP, \code{tidy()} with \code{.data} also builds each term and names
its \code{columns}, as \code{\link[=estimate_mrqap]{estimate_mrqap()}} names them.
}

\section{Reading the model a formula was written for}{

The model a formula was written for is read, in order, from \code{input},
from the class of the formula, such as \code{"ergm_formula"}, or from the
names in the formula:
\itemize{
\item \code{"ergm"} where a term is named only in ergm, such as \code{edges},
\code{mutual}, or \code{nodematch("x")};
\item \code{"dynam"} where a term is named only in goldfish, such as \code{inertia} or
\code{trans};
\item \code{"mrqap"} otherwise.
}

The model that was read is reported. Where a formula mixes ergm's names
with goldfish's, it is read as an ergm formula, and goldfish's names in it
are read as they are in goldfish.
The result of each function carries its model as its class, so that
\code{specify_ergm(specify_mrqap(f))} reads its input without a guess.
}

\section{ergm and infernet}{

ergm names an attribute with a string, and infernet with a bare name.
Both read \verb{levels =} as names or positions. infernet does not read ergm's
functions for \verb{levels =}, such as \code{LARGEST}, or \code{nodemix()}'s \verb{levels =},
which changes the positions of \verb{levels2 =}: such a term is left to ergm,
and an MRQAP drops it. \link{terms_mrqap} gives each term's ergm equivalent.

Read from ergm, \code{nodecov("x")} becomes \code{ego(x) + alter(x)}, and \code{edges}
and \code{density} are the intercept, which an MRQAP always has.
ergm's terms of the outcome's own structure, such as \code{gwesp()}, and its
node fixed effects, \code{sender}, \code{receiver}, and \code{sociality}, are kept for
ergm and dropped for an MRQAP, where \code{(1 | sender)} adds a random
intercept instead.
The left-hand side of an ergm formula names the network rather than a
layer, so where it names no layer or tie attribute of \code{.data}, the
outcome is read as \code{.}.

A formula written for ergm is returned as it is.
Written for ergm, every other term, such as \code{sim()}, \code{indeg()}, or an
interaction, is \code{edgecov()} of the matrix infernet builds, which needs
\code{.data}. With \code{.data}, the formula's environment holds those matrices,
and the outcome as a \code{network} object under its own name, so
\code{ergm::ergm(specify_ergm(f, .data))} fits as it is.
\code{edges} is always added, as ergm's intercept.
\code{(1 | sender)} and \code{(1 | receiver)} become ergm's fixed effects \code{sender}
and \code{receiver}, which are the nearest ergm has, and the fixed effects
\verb{| sender + receiver} become the same terms.
A cognitive social structure or a list of networks aborts, since ergm
fits one report of one network.
}

\section{goldfish and infernet}{

goldfish's terms are infernet's where the two packages share them, as
\link{terms_mrqap} lists.
\code{sim()} is 1 - |d| / range in infernet and -|d| in goldfish,
which changes the scale of its coefficient but not its test, and
\code{tertius()} and \code{tertius_diff()} leave out the sender in infernet and
include it in goldfish. Both are kept and named.

For an MRQAP, goldfish's endogenous effects, such as \code{cycle},
\code{common_sender}, \code{common_receiver}, and \code{four}, read the outcome's own
ties, which permuting the outcome would not permute with them, so they are
dropped. So are goldfish's closures through two layers and the DyNAM-i
statistics of groups. \code{inertia}, \code{recip}, and \code{trans} read the outcome's
previous wave where \code{.data} is a longitudinal network, and fit there; on a
cross-sectional network, \code{inertia} and \code{trans} are dropped too.

goldfish names the side of \code{indeg()} and \code{outdeg()} with \verb{type =}, and
reads the receiver for both by default, where infernet's \code{outdeg()} reads
the sender. A goldfish formula is read as goldfish reads it:
\code{outdeg(friends)} becomes \code{outdeg(friends, node = "alter")}, with a
warning, and \verb{type =} becomes \verb{node =}. \code{indeg} or \code{outdeg} on its own
reads the outcome's own degree in goldfish, and is dropped for an MRQAP
and for ergm. goldfish's older formulas name a node attribute with its
table of nodes, as in \code{ego(actors$floor)}, which is read as \code{ego(floor)},
since the network holds its nodes' attributes.
}

